-- Doc 18 §2.4's `data`, `newtype` and `type`, checked by compiling and
-- running the generated Rust: a declaration lowered to the wrong Rust shape
-- does not compile at all.
--
-- What this corpus can hold is bounded by its own strictness — it requires
-- rustc to emit **nothing**, so every declared thing has to be used. The
-- record struct therefore lives in `examples/record.sdx`, which constructs
-- one; and a tuple *variant*'s field needs a constructor pattern with a
-- binder — `Key c -> …` — which is doc 04's plan 33, the full pattern
-- grammar, so an unread field would be a `dead_code` warning.
--
-- `crates/voltaic-emit/tests/data.rs` covers the record variant. This file
-- covers what runs: unit variants, a constructor in scope under its own name,
-- a tuple struct whose field *is* read — one spelled `newtype` and one
-- spelled `data`, which is plan 53's distinction — an alias, and `deriving`.

-- Several constructors make an `enum`, and a constructor is in scope under
-- its own name as it is in Haskell — which is what keeps the `case` arm
-- below a match rather than an irrefutable binding.
data Event
  = Click
  | Close
  deriving (Debug)

-- A single-field tuple struct, twice, because the two keywords no longer
-- agree — plan 53 leaf 3. `newtype` is the single-field case, so it carries
-- `#[repr(transparent)]`; `data` promises nothing about layout and does not.
--
-- This comment used to say they "lower identically here, because Rust has one
-- such form and not two". The second half is true and the first was the
-- reason to stop: a keyword that means exactly what another keyword means is
-- a keyword with nothing to say, and three further doc comments described the
-- distinction as meaningful while the emitter ignored it. `tests/golden/data.xrs`
-- is where the difference is now visible.
newtype Meters = Meters f64

data Celsius = Celsius f64

-- The one declaration form that is already Rust.
type Depth = f64

-- A constructor is applied by juxtaposition, like any other function.
describe : Event -> &'static str
describe e = case e of
  Close -> "close"
  _ -> "click"

depth : Meters -> Depth
depth m = m.0

-- The `data` half of the contrast. Its field is read for the same reason
-- every other declared thing here is used: this corpus requires rustc to emit
-- nothing, so an unread field is a `dead_code` warning and a red gate.
warmer : Celsius -> f64
warmer c = c.0 + 1.0

main : ()
main =
  println! \-> "#{(describe Close)}"
  println! \-> "#{(describe Click)}"
  println! \-> "#{(depth (Meters 5.0))}"
  println! \-> "#{(depth (Meters 1.5))}"
  println! \-> "#{(warmer (Celsius 20.0))}"
  ()
